ing direction. Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read: \"Sort sheet group-by controls: the grouping direction is now initialised from a new fallback value.\" This is a hint that in the sort sheet, when you change the group-by, the direction gets a fallback. The bug might be that when changing grouping to a type, the direction is initialized to a value that is ***the same as the previous grouping's direction*** or something, causing wrong display. We observed different directions per type though (Asc/Desc as sensible). Hmm.\n\nLet me carefully re-run the sequence and log:\n- Initial (before any changes): due date Desc.\n- After choosing By priority: Desc.\n- None: n/a.\n- By start date: Asc.\n- Created: Desc.\n- By due date: Asc.\n- (later) By last modified: Desc.\n- List: Asc.\n- None: n/a.\n\nIf fallback were purely by type:\ndue date=Asc, priority=Desc, start date=Asc, created=Desc, last modified=Desc, list=Asc. All sensible. So the new fallback seems correct.\n\nUnless the expected fallback for due date is Descending (matching initial saved state) and now it's Ascending, and the change is itself the bug? No, asc is more sensible.\n\nHmm, maybe I'm wrong that all sensible. What about \"List\" grouping: if you group by list, within each list the tasks are sorted by the sorting criterion; the group order by list name. Ascending = A→Z. Reasonable.\n\nOK, maybe the injected bug for grouping is something else entirely: e.g., changing group-by to \"None\" leaves the \"Grouping\" row clickable but the direction toggle doing nothing; or the group-by selection list is missing an option (e.g., \"By tag\"?). The available options: None, By due date, By start date, By priority, By last modified, Created, List. Tasks.org also supports grouping by tag? Actually maybe not. Hmm.\n\nOr maybe \"By last modified\" and \"Created\" should show a specific direction default.\n\nLet me try a different tack: check the app's sort sheet \"Completed\" direction toggling and \"Show completed\" etc. Those are also part of sorting/grouping options. Maybe a bug is injected there. But the change note specifically mentions group-by controls.\n\nWait—maybe the direction fallback bug manifests when you first select a grouping from the list: the direction shown in the sheet is correct, but the *actual list* uses the old direction until you close and reopen the sheet. We tested only after closing. Let me test: from None, select By due date (fallback Asc), immediately close, list should be ascending. Earlier when I did: selected By due date (Asc) then closed → list was ascending. Yes worked.\n\nHmm.\n\nLet me consider that maybe the injected bugs are:\n- Search: live refresh has a bug where results are stale for the *first* character? We saw after typing \"m\", results updated immediately. But note: typing via type_text may have inserted the whole word quickly. We observed after \"m\" 2 results. Good.\n- Weekday order: maybe the bug is that it's ordered by locale's first day but *not* localizing the letters; e.g., in a non-English locale the letters would be wrong. Not testable.\n- Group direction: maybe the bug is a single fallback constant, e.g., \"ASC\" for all, but we saw DESC for priority. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I mis-read: maybe the \"By priority\" fallback showing Desc was actually because the *previous* setting was Desc and the fallback wasn't applied because the preference existed? Ugh, we can't distinguish.\n\nLet me try to find a scenario where the direction is clearly wrong/unexpected. Test \"By start date\" grouping with tasks that have start dates...none. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: create tasks with start dates/priorities to test group order? That's a lot.\n\nMaybe better: check persistence of grouping settings across app restart. Close app (open another app? we can only open the app under test). \"open_app brings app to foreground (does not reset data)\" – not kill. We could press HOME then open_app to background/foreground. Or use APP_SWITCH? We can't forc