{"type": "thinking", "text": "Whoa! Typing \"%\" shows **all tasks** (no filtering). Is that a bug? If search treats \"%\" as SQL wildcard, it matches everything, which is arguably wrong UX but could also be \"no match → show all\"? Let's compare: typing \"zzzz\" showed \"There are no tasks here.\" So \"%\" matching all tasks indicates the query \"%\" is treated as a wildcard (SQL LIKE '%' matches any string). That's an unescaped wildcard bug! Similarly \"_\" would match any single character, so \"_\" would match titles with at least one char → all tasks.\n\nWait, but maybe the app intentionally shows all tasks for queries with no alphanumeric characters? Hmm. Let's test \"_\" and a literal \"%\" that exists nowhere. Also test \"a%\" or \"%milk\". If \"%\" alone matches all, that confirms wildcard behavior. But is this the injected search bug? It's not about \"refreshed as each character typed\" though.\n\nHmm, the change note is about live refresh. The benchmark may have also injected an unrelated search bug. But this wildcard behavior may be pre-existing in Tasks.org (it uses SQLite LIKE with FTS?). Actually Tasks.org search uses a `TaskDao` with `title LIKE ?`? If it concatenates \"%\" + query + \"%\" without escaping, then a query containing \"%\" would act as wildcard. Many apps have this. Could be pre-existing, not the injected bug. But it's a defensible bug report: searching \"%\" should return tasks literally containing \"%\", not all tasks. Hmm. Is it worth reporting? The benchmark grades on finding the *intended* bugs; false positives might be penalized? It says \"a quote that was never on screen gets the report rejected\" — reports are validated by a developer. An unexpected \"%\" all-match could be accepted as a bug or rejected as not-a-bug/by design.\n\nLet me test \"_\" and a query like \"m%lk\" to see wildcard semantics. If \"m%lk\" matches \"milk\"? Actually \"m%lk\" would match \"milk\" (m + any + lk) if wildcard. Let's test. Also test escaped/literal search for \"%\": no task contains %, so expected no results.\n\nBut hold on: maybe the app's search with no valid tokens displays all tasks (like whitespace). \"%\" may be stripped as non-alphanumeric, resulting in empty query → all tasks. That's plausible too (tokenizer strips punctuation). Then it's not wildcard. Test \"m%lk\": if it matches \"milk\", wildcard/ignored punctuation; if no results, then \"%\" is stripped.\n\nLet me test \"m%lk\"."}
