{"type": "thinking", "text": "Very interesting! In Spanish, the weekday chips are: D L M M J V S (element labels: [4] D, [5] L, [6] M, [7] M, [8] J, [9] V, [10] S).\n\nSpanish weekdays: lunes(L), martes(M), miércoles(X or M), jueves(J), viernes(V), sábado(S), domingo(D).\n\nIf ordered Monday-first: L M X J V S D. If Sunday-first: D L M X J V S.\n\nObserved: D L M M J V S. That's Sunday-first (D=domingo, L=lunes, M=martes, M=miércoles, J=jueves, V=viernes, S=sábado). So it's still Sunday-first even though Spanish locale's first day of week is **Monday**! This is the bug! The weekday choices are NOT ordered by the locale's first day of the week — they're stuck on Sunday-first (likely because the app uses en-US or a hardcoded Sunday first). \n\nWait, hold on. Is the per-app locale actually Spanish? The UI is Spanish. But `Locale.getDefault()` might still be en-US if per-app language is applied via AppCompat but `WeekFields.of(Locale.getDefault())` might return the system locale (en-US), not the app locale. Hmm. In Android per-app language, `Locale.getDefault()` returns the app locale (since Android 13, LocaleManager sets the app's locale and it affects Locale.getDefault() within the app). Actually, for per-app languages, the app's Configuration locale is changed; `Locale.getDefault()` returns the *system* default, not the app's configuration locale, unless the app calls `Locale.setDefault`. Hmm. The correct way is `context.getResources().getConfiguration().getLocales().get(0)`.\n\nSo the intended fix \"ordered by the locale's first day of the week\" — which locale? The app's display locale should be the configuration locale (Spanish). If the implementation uses `Locale.getDefault()` (system en-US), the order stays Sunday-first even though the app displays Spanish. That would be the bug! But wait, the device *system* locale is en-US (the picker said \"System default\" = English US). With per-app language Spanish, the app's locale is Spanish. A correct implementation using the app resources' locale would order Monday-first. The observed Sunday-first indicates it uses the system locale (en-US) or hardcodes Sunday.\n\nBut hold on — is the date picker also Sunday-first now? Let me check the date picker in Spanish. If the date picker (Material) uses the app configuration locale, it should be Monday-first. If it also shows Sunday-first, then maybe the app's effective locale for date formatting is still en-US in some APIs while strings are Spanish. Let me check the due date picker: open it (cancel custom first). Actually, the date picker is a different component; it might use `Locale.getDefault()` too or the configuration locale.\n\nBut more importantly: the task's change note says \"weekday choices are now ordered by the locale's first day of the week.\" The expected behavior in Spanish (Monday-first) should be L M X J V S D. Observed D L M M J V S — Sunday-first. This looks like the injected bug! \n\nBut wait — maybe the app language switch didn't actually change the *app's* locale used by date/time functions, only translations. The due date in the list is Spanish formatted (\"jueves\", \"24 de noviembre\"), which uses the configuration locale. So the app's date formatting uses Spanish. The weekday chips use Spanish abbreviations (D L M M J V S), so they also use Spanish weekday names. But the *order* starts Sunday. For the locale es-US (Spanish US), what is the first day of week? Spanish (United States) — CLDR: es-US first day of week is Sunday? Hmm! Wait. es-US might use Sunday as first day because it's US region! Let me check CLDR: For es-US, the first day of week might be Sunday (US convention), whereas es-ES is Monday. Hmm! That's a crucial nuance.\n\nCLDR week data: firstDay for \"es\" (Spain) is Monday. For \"es-US\" (Spanish US), it might be Sunday because region US. Actually CLDR's `firstDay` is specified per territory; for US it's Sunday. es-US uses region US, so first day = Sunday. Hmm! So choosing \"Español (Estados Unidos)\" might still be Sunday-first. That would make the observed Sunday-first correct!\n\nI need a locale that is definitively Monday-first: e.g., \"Español (España)\" or \"Català\" or \"Deutsch\" or \"Français\" or \"English (United Kingdom)\". The list had \"Català\" and \"Bosanski\". Let me switch to a locale with Monday-first, e.g., Català (ca-ES, Monday-first), or better \"English (United Kingdom)\" (en-GB, Monday-first). Let me check the language list for English (United Kingdom) — scroll the all-languages list. Or use Català which was visible. Catalan first day: Monday. The app would show Catalan UI; weekday abbreviations: dl, dt, dc, dj, dv, ds, dg. If ordered Monday-first: D L M X J V S? Catalan: dilluns (dl), dimarts (dt), dimecres (dc), dijous (dj), divendres (dv), dissabte (ds), diumenge (dg). Single-letter abbreviations might all be \"D\" for many, confusing. Hmm.\n\nBetter: English (United Kingdom) — abbreviations M T W T F S S. First day Monday. That's clear. Let me scroll the language list to find it. Or \"Français\" — L M M J V S D (Monday-first), distinct. French abbreviations: L M M J V S D. That would be clear: if Monday-first, starts L; if Sunday, starts D. And French is definitely Monday-first (fr-FR). But is \"Français\" in the list? Likely yes (Tasks.org translations). I can scroll.\n\nAlternatively \"Deutsch\" — Mo Di Mi Do Fr Sa So; but single-letter would be M D M D F S S. Ugh.\n\nFrench single letters: L M M J V S D. Great discriminator. Let me switch to Français.\n\nActually, simpler: keep Spanish but choose \"Español (España)\" if available. The suggested list had \"Español (Estados Unidos)\" only. All languages likely includes \"Español (España)\"? Android's language picker lists locales per language with variants; the \"All languages\" list shows \"Español\" maybe then variants. Hmm.\n\nLet me go to the language picker again and search. Navigate: Cancel custom dialog (element 20), back to editor, back to list, top-right settings, Look and